What is DAST in DevSecOps ⁉️ 

✅ DAST stands for Dynamic Application Security Testing. The simplest way to understand it is that it's an automated "live attack simulation" on a running application.

Here is a simpler breakdown:
1️⃣  The Core Idea: Testing from the Outside 🕵️
DAST (Dynamic Testing) Tests the app while it's running.Tests the app while it's sitting still (the source code).

DAST tools don't look at your source code (the blueprint of the application).
Instead, they act like a hacker or a normal user, sending various requests and malicious inputs (like trying to inject code into a search box) to the running application.
It focuses on finding vulnerabilities that are only visible when the application is actively running and responding to external requests.

2️⃣  The Simple Analogy 🏡
Imagine your application is a brand-new house.
Static Testing (SAST) is like looking at the blueprints and checking for flaws in the wiring diagrams or structural plans.
Dynamic Testing (DAST) is like hiring a professional security consultant to walk around the finished, locked house and try to:
Open doors and windows (even if they look secure).
See if they can climb through a ventilation shaft.
Test the alarm system by setting it off.
DAST tests what is exposed and how the system behaves under stress.

3️⃣  What DAST is Good At 🎯
Because DAST tests the live application through its external interfaces (like a web browser or API endpoints), it is excellent at finding:
Configuration errors in the live environment.
Injection Flaws (like SQL Injection or Cross-Site Scripting).
Authentication and Authorization issues (e.g., can a low-level user access an admin page).
Vulnerabilities that arise from the interaction of different components at runtime.
